WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.560
How do you feel when you have a conversation
with somebody that can recite something you

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.890
told them a year, two years, three years ago
and you're saying to yourself, "How does this

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:13.110
person even remember the details of what we
talked about two years ago?"

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.990
And the most important question is, how do
you feel when someone can actually do that?

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:22.029
Do you sit there and like the person more,
respect them more because they're actually

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:24.170
paying attention to what you're saying?

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.439
So Mario asked me this question yesterday.

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:33.020
He said, "Hey Pat, is there a way we can talk
about how to retain information that entrepreneurs

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.010
can benefit from?"

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.500
We grabbed a paper and pen and came up with
12 different things that I'll talk about here

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.400
in this video how you can improve your memory.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:47.640
I'm not talking about the gifted people who
have photographic memories or eidetic memory.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.360
I'm not talking about that.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.370
I'm not talking about a Nikola Tesla who had
an incredible memory or Teddy Roosevelt that

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.750
could read the paper and recite the entire
thing back to you.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.400
That's a certain gift that a human being has.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.400
Certain people have that.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.740
I'm talking basic things that you can do to
improve your memory that can help you out

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.860
in many parts of your life, whether it's business,
whether it's relationship, whether it's any

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.920
type of thing that you're doing that has to
do with people.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.170
So let's get right into it.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:23.310
#1: When you do retain information, there's
a couple of things you've got to keep in mind.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.520
The first thing is this.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.870
Think about information that you yourself
remember.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.390
Think about information that you yourself
remember.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.760
Whatever that information may be.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.680
Then ask the question, why do you remember
that?

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:38.810
So stay here.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.330
It's typically two reasons.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.030
Why do you remember that information?

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:47.380
For instance, some people can remember sports
stats like this [snap].

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.570
Some people can remember a quote from a president
like this [snap].

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:56.800
Some people can remember mathematical formulas
like this [snap].

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.000
But some people can remember some bad times
on what happened with details.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.360
Some people can remember certain laws or speeding
limits.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.620
How do you remember that?

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.040
How is that possible?

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.040
How do you remember?

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.060
How do you recall those types of things?

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:17.840
So our brain typically, the general side,
we remember and retain information that interests

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.790
us or information that affects us.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:26.770
For instance, you will know your tax rate
if it affects you after paying $63,000 in

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.310
taxes in a year, hypothetically.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.400
That affects you.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.940
You're not interested in it, but it affects
you, so you know that number.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.900
Or you have an interest in a video game and
you remember certain secrets to a video game

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.250
that you need to do this because it interests
you.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.510
So what does that have to do with improving
information?

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.830
Well, when you are talking to somebody, and
you want to retain the information, you've

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:56.160
got to make sure that you're either interested
in the human being or the content or whatever

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.110
book you're reading or videos you're studying,
classroom you're studying, or you've got to

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.290
know at some content that affects you.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:06.790
If it's not one of those two things, you're
generally not going to remember that content

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:07.790
for too long.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:08.790
#2: Let me give you the second on here.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.650
The second one is repetition.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:16.410
Repetition could be the way we remember songs.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.360
I have a certain song that I like and I listen
to it over and over again and all of a sudden

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.020
I know the lyrics to it, right, whatever the
lyrics are.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:29.220
The same way you and I can remember certain
lyrics to a song is the same way you and I

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:37.590
can remember a verse to a book or a formula
or a quote or whatever it is or an event or

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:42.790
details of what a person said, because you
keep repeating it and then it stays [snap].

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.000
You keep repeating it and it stays.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.820
So for instance, today I talked to this, this,
this, he said this, he said this, he said

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.840
this, she said this . . . - you're repeating
it.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:51.840
And he said this.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:52.840
His name is this.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:53.840
His name is this.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.840
His name is this.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.020
Repeat, repeat, repeat, repeat, repeat, and
then you link, you link, you link, you link.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.709
So repeat, link, repeat, link, and then it
stays.

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:02.790
So that's the second thing.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.810
#3: Here's the third thing I like to do.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.040
Let's just say that you learned something
just now.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.310
You read something, you learned it.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.990
What do you do about it?

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.690
Debate it with a group of two or three friends,
or family members.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.090
What do I mean by debate?

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.519
For instance, what could be a debate?

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:23.780
Okay, you just learned that unemployment rates
in Panama are 2.3%.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.470
You just learned that today.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.910
You go and sit there with - let's just say
that Mario, Paul and I say, "Hey, why do you

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:33.750
think Panama's unemployment rate is 2.3%?"
Paul gives his opinion.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:34.940
Mario gives his opinion.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.050
I give my opinion.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.050
I disagree.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:38.050
No I agree.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:39.050
No I disagree.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.050
No, it's because of this.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.050
No, it's because of that.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.400
Then we may do a little bit of research, then
all of a sudden that 2.3% stays in the mind,

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.620
because you debated it.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.510
And I'm talking debate with people that are
not combative.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:51.990
They aren't people that just want to win everything.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.800
Debate because you're trying to learn.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.690
So take whatever you learned, debate it, does
this work, boom, boom, boom, boom, it's going

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:04.130
to stay, because there's a lot of energy you
have to use to justify why you believe that

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.590
and you have to think about why and it stays,
the information stays.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:12.090
#4: The fourth point that I'm going to tell
you about.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.660
Share it with other people.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.170
So let's just say you watched a video.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.670
And one of the videos is people ask me what
type of mentors you should have.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.460
I said TEA theory.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.580
So what is TEA theory?

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.740
T stands for theory
E stands for experience

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.250
A stands for application

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:33.970
So, when you want to have a mentor there's
three different levels of mentors.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:37.450
There are people that are just theory, like
a teacher who's read a lot of books and they're

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:40.950
going to give you their theories that they've
read and their own theories.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.020
Second type of mentor could be a person who
has experience.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.919
They work closely with Michael Jordan and
they know Michael Jordan's habits, so they

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:51.240
can tell you Mike's like this, Mike's like
that or president's like this or this CEO

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.240
was like this.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.240
Great.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.320
And the third one is a person you actually
get mentored by, Michael Jordan, because they're

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.729
applying what they know that they're sharing
with you.

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:02.830
And the best type of mentor is the trifecta.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.009
Okay, let's take that subject that you and
I just shared.

00:06:05.009 --> 00:06:09.639
It's one of my videos that I've done, how
to choose the right mentor.

00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:14.300
Now, take that and share it with a handful
of people today.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.240
Hey, You know what's the best type of mentor?

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.730
TEA -- what does that mean?

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:18.730
Let me explain it to you.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:23.069
TEA stands for theory, experience, and application.

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:25.849
So let me explain to you what I mean by that.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:26.849
. . That person learned it.

00:06:26.849 --> 00:06:28.430
You're teaching, you're sharing, that person
learned it.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:29.860
Mario, did you know what a tea theory is?

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:30.860
No, I don't.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:32.380
Let me explain to you what a tea theory is.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:33.380
Tea theory is with mentors.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.400
Boom, boom, boom.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.550
T is for theory, e is for experiment, a is
for application.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:38.550
Now watch this.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.200
I've done that three times to you, okay?

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.660
If I were to ask you to repeat to me what
TEA stands for, I'm willing to bet you know

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:46.790
exactly what it stands for.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:47.900
Watch, go ahead and do it.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:48.900
Go ahead and do it.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.650
I'm going to give you five seconds.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.139
What does TEA stand for?

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:57.020
T. . .. . E . . . . A. . . . That's right.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.240
Theory, experiment, application.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.240
You just did it.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.240
Why?

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.570
I'm sharing it with you.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.740
So if you want to retain the content that
you're learning here today, make notes of

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.590
it, go share it with somebody else.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:13.800
It will increase the likelihood of you retaining
the information in this video.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.720
#5: Teach on it.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.990
Don't just share it, teach on it.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:18.130
For instance, teach on it is this.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.610
And he was explaining how.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:21.789
. . and there's this and there's that.

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:25.740
It's different from sharing because sharing
could be, "Did you know that Michael Jordan

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.370
